demo code for tutoring lessons

lesson 1: screen saver (screen_saver.py)

Teaching Plan: Building a Bouncing Text Screensaver
Step 1: Setup & Initialization (5-10 minutes)
Start with the absolute basics:

Import pygame
Initialize pygame with pygame.init()
Create a window with pygame.display.set_mode()
Set the window title
Goal: Student sees a blank window and understands the "setup" phase

Step 2: Draw Static Text (10 minutes)
Build on Step 1:

Create a font object
Render text as an image
Position it in the center using get_rect()
Draw it to the screen with screen.blit()
Goal: Static "Hello!" text appears centered on screen

Step 3: Create a Game Loop (10 minutes)
Introduce the main loop concept:

Explain why games need a loop (constantly updating)
Add event handling (quit button)
Add FPS limiting with clock.tick()
Redraw every frame
Goal: Text stays visible, window is responsive (can close it)

Step 4: Move the Text (15 minutes)
Introduce velocity and animation:

Create x_velocity and y_velocity variables
Explain pixels-per-second concept
Calculate dt (delta time) to make movement frame-rate independent
Update position: text_rect.x += x_velocity * dt
Goal: Text moves across screen smoothly

Step 5: Add Bouncing (15 minutes)
Introduce collision detection:

Check if text hits left/right walls: text_rect.left < 0 or text_rect.right > SCREEN_WIDTH
Reverse velocity when hit: x_velocity *= -1
Repeat for top/bottom walls
Goal: Text bounces off all 4 edges

Step 6: Add Color Changes (10 minutes)
Add visual interest:

Import random
Generate random RGB colors when text bounces
Re-render text with new color each frame
Goal: Text changes color every time it hits a wall

Final Project: Have students modify it:
Change the message
Adjust velocities (faster/slower)
Change starting color
Add multiple bouncing objects
Change window size
Key Concepts to Emphasize:
The Game Loop: Everything happens in the loop (60 times per second)
Delta Time (dt): Why we multiply velocity by dt for smooth motion
Rectangles: How get_rect() helps with positioning and collision
Frame Rate Independence: Same speed on fast and slow computers
This progression goes from "watching paint dry" → "static text" → "interactive program" → "animation" → "game logic" — keeping students engaged at each step!